hadiths[9]{hadithnumber,text}:
"18187","Waki narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jaad, from Abu Kabshah al-Anmari who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The parable of this nation is the parable of four men: A man whom Allah has given wealth and knowledge, so he acts with it in his wealth, spending it in its right; and a man whom Allah has given knowledge but not given wealth, so he says: If I had like what this one has, I would act in it like what he does. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: So they are equal in reward. And a man whom Allah has given wealth but not given knowledge, so he stumbles with it, spending it in other than its right; and a man whom Allah has given neither wealth nor knowledge, so he says: If I had wealth like this one, I would act in it like what he does. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: So they are equal in sin."
"18188","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jaad, and I heard it from him, narrating from Abu Kabshah al-Anmari of Ghatafan, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: The parable of my nation is the parable of four men, and mentioned the hadith, except that he said: A man whom Allah has given wealth but not given knowledge, so he stumbles with it, neither maintaining kinship with it nor giving its due in it."
"18189","Abdullah ibn al-Walid al-Adani narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jaad, from Abu Kabshah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, struck the parable of this nation as the parable of four men, and mentioned the hadith."
"18190","Rawh narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Sulayman who said: I heard Salim ibn Abi al-Jaad say: I heard Abu Kabshah al-Anmari say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The parable of my nation is the parable of four, and mentioned the hadith."
"18191","Abdur-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Muawiyah, meaning Ibn Salih, from Azhar ibn Saeed al-Harazi who said: I heard Abu Kabshah al-Anmari say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was sitting among his Companions, then he entered, then came out having bathed. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, something happened? He said: Yes, so-and-so woman passed by me, and desire for women fell into my heart, so I came to one of my wives and had her. So do likewise, for it is among the best of your deeds to come to the lawful."
"18192","Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-Masudi informed us, from Ismail ibn Awsat, from Muhammad ibn Abi Kabshah al-Anmari, from his father who said: When it was the expedition of Tabuk, people hastened to the people of al-Hijr, entering upon them. That reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he called out among the people: Prayer in congregation. He said: So I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was holding his camel, saying: Why do you enter upon a people whom Allah is angry with? A man among them called to him: We marvel at them, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Shall I not warn you of something more marvelous than that: A man from among yourselves who informs you of what was before you and what will be after you. So be upright and steadfast, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not care for your punishment at all, and there will come a people who will not defend themselves with anything."
"18193","Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Kabshah al-Anmari, from his father who said: When it was the expedition of Tabuk, a people hastened to the people of al-Hijr, entering upon them, and mentioned its meaning."
"18194","Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Numayr narrated to us, Ubadah ibn Muslim narrated to us, Yunus ibn Khabbab narrated to me, from Saeed Abu al-Bakhtari al-Tai, from Abu Kabshah al-Anmari who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Three things I swear upon, and I narrate to you a hadith, so memorize it. He said: As for the three upon which I swear: No charity ever decreases the wealth of a servant, no servant is wronged with a wrong and is patient over it but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, increases him in honor thereby, and no servant opens a door of begging but Allah opens for him a door of poverty. And as for what I narrate to you as a hadith, so memorize it, he said: This world is only for four men: A servant whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has granted wealth and knowledge, so he fears his Lord in it, maintains his kinship in it, and knows the right of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in it. He said: So this is in the best stations. He said: And a servant whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has granted knowledge but not granted wealth. He said: So he says: If I had wealth, I would act as so-and-so does. He said: So their reward is equal. He said: And a servant whom Allah has granted wealth but not granted knowledge, so he stumbles in his wealth without knowledge, neither fearing his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, in it, nor maintaining his kinship in it, nor knowing the right of Allah in it. So this is in the vilest stations. He said: And a servant whom Allah has granted neither wealth nor knowledge, so he says: If I had wealth, I would act as so-and-so does. He said: It is his intention, so their sin therein is equal."
"18195","Yazid ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Al-Zubaydi narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Saad, from Abu Amir al-Hawzani, from Abu Kabshah al-Anmari, that he came to him and said: Lend me your horse for breeding, for I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever lends a mare and it foals for him, he shall have like the reward of seventy horses carried upon in the path of Allah."
